# Digest manifests are READ BY `sha256sum -c`, which treats a trailing CR as
# part of the FILENAME. On a core.autocrlf=true clone — every Windows checkout
# in this repo — a CRLF manifest makes EVERY entry fail to open, and the
# reproducer reports that identically to real drift: "REFUSING: sources have
# drifted ... the certification record has LAPSED". A certification tool that
# cannot distinguish "the bytes moved" from "your git normalised my line
# endings" is worse than no tool.
#
# Pinned by EXTENSION, not by filename: the first version of this rule named
# A11_SNAPSHOT.sha256 explicitly, which meant the very next manifest — under any
# other name — silently reintroduced the bug. Raised by session nexusqa-9e,
# who found the same class of defect in squid.conf, where it did not fail
# loudly but PASSED misleadingly.
#
# Scoped to certification/ deliberately. Other squads' files are not ours to
# re-normalise.
*.sha256  text eol=lf
